Hi,

This came up in a working session and nobody had the same definition.

During service degradation, repeated attempts can inflate volume and obscure =
the number of customers actually affected.

I do not want a dashboard that simply counts the outcome. Start with the comp=
eting explanations: channel switching follows a timeout; automatic retry and =
human retry are being mixed; or customers retry the same action within minute=
s.

Failure anecdotes are beginning to drive decisions, although retries and late=
 posting may be making the problem look larger than the affected-customer pop=
ulation. This has returned because the previous answer was useful but not str=
ong enough to become a standing rule. Use March 2021 as the new evidence poin=
t rather than recycling the earlier conclusion.=20

What we need to decide is how monitoring should count affected customers and =
when retry controls should activate. Please bring a short decision pack with =
no more than five slides and an attached evidence table, including a few case=
s we can trace from source to conclusion.

The available evidence is transaction-level timestamps, channels, amounts, st=
atuses and error context; ATM attempts, host responses, PIN attempts, blocked=
-card state and latency. Without application session IDs, use account, amount=
, channel and time proximity as an explicit matching heuristic.

Thank you
